WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.920
So how's Democrats yesterday, as we discussed,
they were about to do release the resolution

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.970
to formalize the impeachment inquiry into
Donald Trump.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:17.670
So understand that so far they've been pre-interviewing
witnesses in private, in accordance with the

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:18.670
law.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.100
By the way, as I will shortly remind you in
the next segment, a very interesting segment

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.320
upcoming.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.949
This is now an impeachment inquiry resolution.

00:00:25.949 --> 00:00:31.820
The house is expected to vote on it Thursday
and it outlines the process that is going

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:38.510
to be taking place to inquire about whether
Donald Trump should be impeached by the house

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:43.690
of representatives ultimately subsequently
to be voted on by the Senate.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:45.020
What does the resolution say?

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:50.780
Well, for starters, at its conclusion, the
inquiry is going to create a public rec report

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:51.780
of their findings.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:58.250
And, uh, this is important, uh, in the sense
that it is one of the areas that has been

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.910
heavily criticized by Republicans, that everything
is happening quote in secret.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.929
Well, in reality, nothing has actually happened
yet.

00:01:04.929 --> 00:01:07.049
There's just been pre-interviewing of witnesses.

00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:11.310
But at the conclusion of this inquiry, there
will be a public report that is going to be

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:12.310
issued.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:16.480
Now importantly to Donald Trump, who as you
may know, has been complaining about, there's

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.749
no due process.

00:01:17.749 --> 00:01:22.490
I'm not getting any due process here, even
though we simply didn't get to that point

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:23.490
yet.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.259
Nothing wrong has happened so far.

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:31.569
The resolution outlines the due process that
Donald Trump is going to, to, to get, and

00:01:31.569 --> 00:01:34.579
you can make the argument that it's actually
generous to Donald Trump.

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:41.619
The resolution outlines some new due process
rights for the president of the United States.

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:48.889
Overall in a sort of balanced, sober rational
analysis, there's really nothing controversial

00:01:48.889 --> 00:01:50.469
in the resolution whatsoever.

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:55.079
It's very similar to the process that was
employed, uh, during bill Clinton's impeachment

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:56.239
back in 1998.

00:01:56.239 --> 00:02:00.499
The difference being that with bill Clinton,
there was this independent council report.

00:02:00.499 --> 00:02:04.249
Remember this guy Ken Starr, who was brought
in to investigate bill Clinton.

00:02:04.249 --> 00:02:10.849
Uh, there was a report which came, which presented
was presented to the house committees involved

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:14.900
by the independent council, uh, specifically
through the judiciary committee.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.469
This time it's a little bit different.

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:22.750
The house itself will be doing the investigating
and it will be uh, the entity, the judiciary

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:27.599
committee will be the entity issuing the full
report rather than receiving it from an, uh,

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:30.510
a special prosecutor, independent prosecutor.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.909
So now the rubber is sort of going to start
meeting the road.

00:02:33.909 --> 00:02:38.569
And what I mean by that is we're going to
see very quickly if Republicans and Donald

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:44.409
Trump settle down over, there are no guidelines
and no due process because the resolution

00:02:44.409 --> 00:02:47.109
gives them everything that they've been asking
for.

00:02:47.109 --> 00:02:51.689
Now of course there is a chance, call me a
skeptic, call me a cynic.

00:02:51.689 --> 00:02:57.830
There's a chance that it wasn't really about
process and guidelines all along.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:03.939
It was just something that Republicans and
Trump grasped onto like a last desperate straw

00:03:03.939 --> 00:03:06.430
to try to stop what it is that is going on.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:10.879
But in any case, if we go by what they've
said, Republicans have been asking for their

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:13.340
own subpoena power to bring in witnesses.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.499
They are being given that by this resolution.

00:03:15.499 --> 00:03:20.060
Donald Trump has been asking or demanding
or complaining about not having due process.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.260
He is getting that.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.579
We have very clear guidelines here for what
this process is going to look like and what

00:03:24.579 --> 00:03:26.560
the deliverable is going to be.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.480
Deliverable by the way, doesn't have to be
a bad word, although when Donald Trump uses

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.030
it, it often alludes to corruption.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.430
The deliverable in this case will be a report,
a public report.

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:37.019
I'm summarizing the impeachment inquiry.

00:03:37.019 --> 00:03:41.390
Uh, Trump want to do process and the route
resolution gives him that.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:46.099
And it's really important to remember that
the resolution also by the way outlines, and

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:48.290
this is where it starts to get very interesting.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:55.560
Uh, the resolution outlines the possibility
of public hearings, which would be just endlessly

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:56.560
fascinating.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.819
Uh, Congressman Jim McGovern, who's a Democrat
from Massachusetts, chairman of the rules

00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:05.879
committee, he released the resolution yesterday
afternoon.

00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:10.269
I actually bumped into Jim McGovern over the
weekend in North Hampton, Massachusetts at

00:04:10.269 --> 00:04:13.379
the world famous Haymarket cafe.

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:15.079
Very exciting moment for me.

00:04:15.079 --> 00:04:18.500
He clearly forgot who I was and had no recollection
of having met me.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:23.270
But regardless, Jim McGovern releasing for
us yesterday the resolution.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:29.060
So prepare yourselves now for just the new
wave of bad faith arguments from Republicans

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:35.990
about how this is still unfair, how it's still
a witch hunt, how it is still a terrible unfair

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:43.060
example of presidential harassment and also
expect the white house to again start moving

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:48.610
the bar to come up with new excuses for why
they are going to be ignoring and directing

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:53.449
everyone under their purview to ignore all
subpoenas because before it was, there's no

00:04:53.449 --> 00:04:55.530
guidelines, there's no due process.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.200
It's an open ended secret thing that's going
on.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.539
Now we've been told here are the guidelines,
here is the due process and there will be

00:05:02.539 --> 00:05:08.240
a public report after possibly public hearings,
they will come up with something new to justify

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.600
it in their minds and in the minds of their
supporters.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.729
Um, why they are going to not cooperate with
this inquiry.

00:05:15.729 --> 00:05:22.060
What I am most looking forward to is that
in the resolution, this is just great.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:28.280
It mentions 45 minutes of uninterrupted questioning
of witnesses by each side.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:32.819
Democrats would get 45 minutes and then Republicans
would get 45 minutes with witnesses in public

00:05:32.819 --> 00:05:33.870
testimony.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:38.680
If you've watched congressional testimony
before, you probably remember that before

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.449
there is even a prelude and one question asked.

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:46.780
They're low on time because oftentimes each
member of the house will get five minutes.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:48.500
It's just not enough time still.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.290
Sometimes there are fireworks, but it is not
enough time.

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:58.349
45 minutes of uninterrupted questioning of
a single witness, the fireworks and the implosions

00:05:58.349 --> 00:06:03.479
that we could see in 45 minutes of straight
questioning could be absolutely glorious.

00:06:03.479 --> 00:06:05.680
And I am very, very much looking forward to
it.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.720
And this may be, it may be in the end, Republicans
shooting themselves in the foot by demanding

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.990
transparency because now they have it and
it doesn't look like it's the type of transparency

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:19.740
that is going to be particularly good for
the narrative that they have been crafting

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.370
more upcoming on this.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:24.530
I am sure I want to hear from you.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:26.360
How do you think the vote will go in the house?

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.370
Well, the, the most, uh, straight forward
prediction is just a party line, a straight

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:31.680
party line vote.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.981
Um, and what do you expect w if and when these
public impeachment inquiry hearings actually

00:06:35.981 --> 00:06:35.991
get going?

